# mgwalter.msg - Walter at the water plant (mgwalter.ssl). One entry per line; no braces in comments.

# 100 to 102: look (stranger, known) and examine.
{100}{}{You see an old man in a cap, wet to the elbows.}
{101}{}{You see Walter, keeper of the water plant.}
{102}{}{He has a mechanic's hands and a plumber's back. He listens to the machinery the way other men listen for footsteps.}

# 110 to 113: muttering while the pipes leak. 114, 115: once they do not.
{110}{}{Drip, drip, drip. I hear it in my sleep.}
{111}{}{Hold together, you rusted old sow.}
{112}{}{Pressure's down again.}
{113}{}{Sixty-one years old and married to a pump.}
{114}{}{Listen to that. Nothing. Beautiful.}
{115}{}{Pressure's holding. I don't trust it.}

# 150 to 152: message window.
{150}{}{With Walter dead, nobody is going to pay you for the pipes. Whatever the town set aside is probably still on him.}
{151}{}{Walter hands you a lump of junk metal.}
{152}{}{Walter counts two hundred dollars into your hand, twice, to be sure.}
{153}{}{Walter turns out his pockets and comes up short of two hundred. Somebody has been through them. He gives you what is there, and looks at you a little too long.}

# 200 to 207: greetings by state of the job.
{200}{}{Mind the floor, it's wet. Everything's wet. These pipes are older than I am and they leak worse. What do you want?}
{201}{}{Still dripping. The pipes and me both.}
{202}{}{Three leaks, and I can hear every one of them from here. You haven't touched them yet.}
{203}{}{That's one quiet. Two still singing. I can count, you know.}
{204}{}{Just the one left. Don't lose interest now.}
{205}{}{The pressure came up. I felt it in my teeth. Was that you?}
{206}{}{Hold on. The pressure's up and the hissing's stopped, and I never saw your face before. Was that you, out there?}
{207}{}{Pressure's holding. First quiet night I've had since I was fifty. What can I do for you?}

# 210 to 219, 298, 299: hub options (216 to 219 and 299 for low Intelligence).
{210}{}{You look like you could use a hand.}
{211}{}{How do I patch a pipe?}
{212}{}{Where are the leaks, again?}
{213}{}{All three leaks are closed.}
{214}{}{I have scrap metal for you.}
{215}{}{Where does the town's water come from?}
{216}{}{Water go drip.}
{217}{}{Drip still drip.}
{218}{}{Drip stop! Me stop drip!}
{219}{}{Me got junk. You want junk?}
{298}{}{Goodbye, Walter.}
{299}{}{Bye-bye.}

# 220 to 224: the offer.
{220}{}{I could use two knees, is what. Three pipes have split: one by the empty house at the gate, one by the Common House, one on the church wall. Stop all three and there's two hundred in it. Town money.}
{221}{}{Two hundred. I'll see to it.}
{222}{}{Not today.}
{223}{}{Good. Go easy on the church one. The faithful get strange when you hit their wall. Come back when the town's gone quiet.}
{224}{}{On my way.}

# 230 to 232: how to patch a pipe.
{230}{}{If you know pipes, put your Repair know-how to it. If you don't, take a lump of scrap and use it on the split. Don't look at me like that. It's how the whole town got built. Here, start with this.}
{231}{}{Same as I said. Repair it if you know how, or use a lump of scrap on it and hit it till it holds. Moira sells junk, the caravan sells junk, and half of what's lying in the street is junk.}
{232}{}{Right.}

# 240 to 243: the reward.
{240}{}{All three? ...Listen to that. Nothing. That is what two hundred dollars sounds like. Here. And if you find scrap metal out there, I'm buying. Twenty-five apiece.}
{241}{}{You fixed them without being asked. You're a saint or you want something. Either way the town put two hundred aside for the job, and it's yours. Bring me scrap metal and I'll pay for that too.}
{242}{}{I have scrap on me now.}
{243}{}{Glad to help.}

# 250 to 257: scrap metal.
{250}{}{Let's see it. [He weighs a piece in his hand.] That'll be a pipe by Tuesday. Twenty-five apiece. How much are you parting with?}
{251}{}{One piece.}
{252}{}{All of it.}
{253}{}{Take the lot. No charge.}
{254}{}{I'll hang on to it.}
{255}{}{Pleasure. The town thanks you. It won't say so, but I do.}
{256}{}{I have more.}
{257}{}{No charge? Around here? ...I'll remember that. Not many do things worth remembering.}
{258}{}{Still buying scrap?}
{259}{}{Till the day the last pipe rusts through, and then some. Twenty-five apiece. Moira sells it dear. The wasteland gives it away to anybody who'll bend down.}

# 260 to 262: the water, the leaks.
{260}{}{The purifier. Half pre-War pump, half prayer. Not the puddle. Lord, no. Drink from the puddle and you glow, and then you join the church. I've seen it go in that order.}
{261}{}{I'll stick to the pump water.}
{262}{}{One by the empty house, on your left as you come in the gate. One by the Common House, across the pool. One on the church wall. Follow the hissing.}

# 270 to 273: low Intelligence.
{270}{}{Yes. Water go drip. [He holds up three fingers.] Three drips. You make drip stop, I give money. Here. Hit pipe with this.}
{271}{}{Three drips. By gate. By sleep-house. By church. Hit them till they stop.}
{272}{}{Me hit pipe!}
{273}{}{Yes. Junk. Good junk. [He counts the money out for you slowly, one coin at a time, and closes your fingers over it.]}
